  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nDeep upper low is circulating over southern CA and western AZ, and \nHurricane Polo is seen merging into that system via water vapor \nimagery early this afternoon. The main westerlies aloft are along \nthe US-Canada border. The sfc trough axis extends from north TX up \nthrough the Central and Northern Plains with a long fetch of mid-\nlevel moisture streaming from the upper low through the central part \nof the country. As a result of all this, deeper moisture has \ngradually moved from western KS into our forecast area throughout \nthe day. Isentropic ascent coupled with that moisture has brought \nthe first batch of rain into eastern KS this afternoon with weak \ninstability leading to isolated thunderstorms. This rain will \ncontinue through the afternoon and most of the evening, bringing \nmoderate to heavy rain in spotty locations at times. CAMs indicate \nthere may be a short time period later this evening when the rain \nlets up and becomes more scattered in coverage for a bit, but then \nshould pick up again during the overnight hours as the LLJ \nstrengthens. The high end of guidance shows a few isolated pockets \nof 3+ inches could be possible by daybreak, though 1-2\" appears more \nlikely for most. \n\nThere should be a bit more of a lull mid to late morning Wednesday \nbefore the next round of rain moves in for the afternoon and \nevening. The sfc low deepens and matures through the day with the \nwarm front pushing north during this time frame, although there \nremain some differences in the position of the low, which will \nimpact where exactly the dryline and triple point set up. Forecast \nsoundings and hodographs show veering in the low levels through the \nafternoon with CAPE increasing to 1000-1500 J/kg. The instability is \nuncertain and could be lower if clouds and rain linger longer \nthrough the morning without adequate clearing. However, low-level \nshear could still be enough to provide a high-shear low-CAPE setup \nwhich leads to some concern for low-topped supercells. Damaging wind \nwould be the main hazard for any storm that can grow strong enough, \nand given the shear, it would be a short time window but cannot \nentirely rule out the possibility of a quick tornado spinning up or \nhail within any rotating updrafts. However, it should be noted that \ncontinued heavy rain remains the primary concern. There is higher \nconfidence in the heavy rain and flooding threat regardless of the \ninstability, but a more convective nature would add to the potential \nfor intense rainfall rates in an environment that will already have \nPwat between 1.75-2\". \n\nAs our forecast area ends up firmly within the warm sector later \nWednesday night into early Thursday morning, this should allow for \nanother break in the heavier rain especially south of I-70. However, \nareas near the KS/NE border may still see showers and storms at \ntimes through the night, which could exacerbate any flooding that \nmay be ongoing. Getting into daybreak Thursday, another batch of \nrain looks to move from south to north from OK into east central KS. \nThe cold front gradually moves across the area through the day, \nmaking for a wet day until it eventually moves out towards the \nevening, and the upper low fills and merges into the westerlies to \nthe north. By the time all is said and done, most areas should see a \ntotal of 2-4\" with localized amounts around 6\" spread over three days.\nFelt the current Flood Watch well covered the areas where those \nhigh- end amounts look most favorable based on 12z guidance, so have \nmade no changes to headlines at this time. \n\nDrier and cooler weather is favored through the weekend into early \nnext week as zonal flow aloft transitions to northwest flow with a \nstrengthening ridge over the Four Corners region. High temperatures \ndrop to the 60s Friday, then warm up slightly to the lower 70s by \nnext Monday.",
